::ALG2_U05_L03_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>Solve y=x<sup>2</sup> and y=9. {
= (-3,9) and (3,9)#Correct. Set x<sup>2</sup>=9.
~ (9,81)#This is on the parabola but not y=9.
~ (0,9)#This is not on the parabola.
~ (3,3)#This is not on y=9.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>Solve y=x<sup>2</sup> and y=x+6. {
~ (2,8)#This is on the line but not the parabola.
= (-2,4) and (3,9)#Correct. Set x<sup>2</sup>=x+6.
~ (0,6)#This is on the line but not the parabola.
~ No solution#The equations intersect twice.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>Solve y=x<sup>2</sup>-1 and y=3. {
~ (3,8)#This is not on y=3.
~ (0,3)#This is not on the parabola.
= (-2,3) and (2,3)#Correct. Set x<sup>2</sup>-1=3.
~ (2,2)#This is not on y=3.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>Solve y=x<sup>2</sup> and y=-x+2. {
~ (2,4) and (-1,1)#These use sign errors.
~ (0,2) only#This is on the line but not the parabola.
~ No solution#The graphs intersect twice.
= (-2,4) and (1,1)#Correct. Set x<sup>2</sup>=-x+2.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which solution is invalid if x represents a number of items? {
= x=-4#Correct. A negative number of items is not meaningful.
~ x=0#Zero items can be meaningful.
~ x=2#Positive items can be meaningful.
~ x=10#Positive items can be meaningful.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>What should be checked after solving a constrained system? {
~ Only one equation#Every equation matters.
= Each equation and each context restriction#Correct. A feasible solution must satisfy both algebra and context.
~ Only the graph shape#The actual point must be checked.
~ Only the largest x-value#All candidate points require checking.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>A line and circle can have how many intersection points? {
~ Always 1#Different positions are possible.
~ Always 2#A tangent line has one intersection.
= 0, 1, or 2#Correct. A line can miss, touch, or cross a circle twice.
~ Always 0#Intersections may occur.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>A parabola and horizontal line can intersect how many times? {
~ Always 1#Not always.
~ Always 2#A tangent line gives one.
~ Exactly 3#A horizontal line and parabola cannot intersect three times.
= 0, 1, or 2#Correct. The line can miss, touch, or cross the parabola twice.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which point satisfies y=x<sup>2</sup> and y=5x-6? {
= (2,4) and (3,9)#Correct. Set x<sup>2</sup>=5x-6.
~ (1,-1)#This is on the line but not the parabola.
~ (6,36)#This is on the parabola but not the line.
~ No solution#The equations intersect twice.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>Solve y=x<sup>2</sup>+2 and y=6. {
~ (6,38)#This is not on y=6.
= (-2,6) and (2,6)#Correct. Set x<sup>2</sup>+2=6.
~ (0,6)#This is not on the parabola.
~ (2,2)#This is not on y=6.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>In a context, why might a mathematically correct solution be rejected? {
~ It has two coordinates.#Ordered pairs are expected.
~ It is an intersection.#Intersections are usually candidate solutions.
= It violates a constraint such as nonnegative time.#Correct. Context restrictions can limit valid solutions.
~ It was found algebraically.#Algebraic solutions can be valid after checking.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which point satisfies y=x<sup>2</sup>-4 and y=0? {
~ (0,-4)#This is on the parabola but not y=0.
~ (4,0)#This does not satisfy the parabola.
~ No solution#The graph crosses y=0 twice.
= (-2,0) and (2,0)#Correct. Set x<sup>2</sup>-4=0.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>What does feasible mean in a constrained nonlinear system? {
= Satisfies all equations, inequalities, and context limits#Correct. Feasible means all conditions are true.
~ Satisfies one equation only#All conditions matter.
~ Has the greatest y-value#Feasible does not mean maximum.
~ Lies on the x-axis#Feasible points can be elsewhere.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>Solve y=x<sup>2</sup> and y=2x+3. {
~ (1,5)#This is on the line but not the parabola.
= (-1,1) and (3,9)#Correct. Set x<sup>2</sup>=2x+3.
~ (0,3)#This is not on the parabola.
~ No solution#The graphs intersect twice.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which is a solution of y=x<sup>2</sup> and y=16? {
~ (16,4)#This reverses coordinates.
~ (0,16)#This is not on the parabola.
= (4,16)#Correct. 4<sup>2</sup>=16 and y=16.
~ (4,4)#This is not on y=16.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>Why can nonlinear systems have two solutions? {
~ They always include two equations.#Two equations can still have zero or one solution.
~ They always have no solution.#Intersections can exist.
~ Because x and y are both variables.#Variables alone do not determine solution count.
= Curved graphs can intersect another graph more than once.#Correct. Nonlinear shapes can cross a line or curve at multiple points.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>Solve y=x<sup>2</sup> and y=-4. {
= No real solution#Correct. A square cannot equal a negative output on y=x<sup>2</sup>.
~ (2,-4)#This is not on y=x<sup>2</sup>.
~ (-2,-4)#This is not on y=x<sup>2</sup>.
~ (0,-4)#This is not on y=x<sup>2</sup>.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which point satisfies y=x<sup>2</sup>+1 and y=5? {
~ (5,26)#This is not on y=5.
= (-2,5) and (2,5)#Correct. Set x<sup>2</sup>+1=5.
~ (0,5)#This is not on the curve.
~ (2,2)#This is not on y=5.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>What is the graph meaning of no solution? {
~ The graphs are identical.#Identical graphs have many solutions.
~ Every point works.#That means infinitely many solutions.
= The graphs do not intersect in the feasible region.#Correct. No shared feasible point exists.
~ The y-intercepts are equal.#Equal y-intercepts could be one solution.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>If a solution is (4,10), what should be true? {
~ Only x=4 must work.#The y-coordinate matters too.
~ Only y=10 must work.#The x-coordinate matters too.
~ It only needs to be near an intersection.#A solution must satisfy exactly.
= Substituting x=4 and y=10 makes every relation true.#Correct. Both coordinates must satisfy all relations.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>Solve y=x<sup>2</sup>-2x and y=0. {
= (0,0) and (2,0)#Correct. Factor x<sup>2</sup>-2x=0.
~ (1,0)#This is not on the parabola.
~ (2,2)#This is not on y=0.
~ No solution#There are two x-intercepts.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which context restriction is reasonable for height over time? {
~ time t &lt; 0 only#That excludes the actual event after start.
= time t &ge; 0#Correct. Negative time is usually not meaningful after the start.
~ height must be negative#Physical height is usually nonnegative.
~ all negative outputs are valid#Context limits may reject them.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which point satisfies y=x<sup>2</sup> and y=x? {
~ (2,2)#This is not on y=x<sup>2</sup>.
~ (-1,-1)#This is not on y=x<sup>2</sup>.
= (0,0) and (1,1)#Correct. Set x<sup>2</sup>=x.
~ No solution#The graphs intersect twice.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>What should a final answer for a system in context include? {
~ Only a formula#The solved points matter.
~ Only a graph title#The answer needs solutions.
~ Only rejected points#Valid points should be reported.
= Solutions and interpretation of valid points#Correct. Context requires meaning, not only coordinates.
}

::ALG2_U05_L03_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U05_L03_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.SYS.4<br><br>Question:<br>A candidate point fails one equation. What is true? {
= It is not a solution of the system.#Correct. A system solution must satisfy every relation.
~ It is still a solution#Failing one relation invalidates it.
~ It is always feasible#Feasible requires all conditions.
~ It must be a vertex#Failure is unrelated to vertex status.
}

